'''''Dracaena suffruticosa''''', [[Synonym (taxonomy)|synonym]] '''''Sansevieria suffruticosa'''''<ref name=POWO_77182375-1/> is a species of ''[[Dracaena (plant)|Dracaena]]'' native to eastern Africa, from [[Ethiopia]] to [[Malawi]].<ref name=WCSP/> The first description of the species was made in 1915 by [[N. E. Brown]].<ref name=WCSP/><ref>{{Citation |last=Brown |first=Nicholas Edward |title=Sansevieria - a monograph of all the known species (with plates) |volume=1915 |work=[[Kew Bulletin|Kew Bulletin of Miscellaneous Information]] |publisher=Royal Botanic Gardens |year=1915 |edition=202 |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/11492724 }}</ref>

==Description==
It forms a short stem and grows its leaves in a rosette or sometimes in two spiraling rows (spiral distichous). Its spine-tipped leaves are 12-16mm thick, elliptical (rather than round) in cross-section, and pale to dark green depending on sun exposure.

It very closely resembles the smaller related species, ''[[Dracaena serpenta]]'' and the more distichous species ''[[Dracaena phillipsiae]]''.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://plants.jstor.org/compilation/Sansevieria.suffruticosa|title=''Sansevieria suffruticosa'' in Global Plants on JSTOR|website=plants.jstor.org|language=en|access-date=2018-05-27}}</ref> In its native habitat it often grows in dry or rocky ground,<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.ville-ge.ch/musinfo/bd/cjb/africa/details.php?langue=an&id=175006|title=''Sansevieria suffruticosa'' N.E.Br.|website=African Plant Database|access-date=2018-05-27}}</ref> and is highly drought tolerant.

